the sixth column adds one quantum  along y  and one along z  in this second group of three states the  two quanta that we need to add to the  system are shared between two spatial  dimensions  so why do we have so many possibilities  this is actually not a general result  for any three-dimensional system  it is a special feature of the quantum  harmonic oscillator  the key is that the energy eigenvalues  of the quantum harmonic oscillator are  equally spaced the energy quantum is  always h bar omega between any two  levels  this means that we can add the exact  same energy by adding two quanta along  some particular direction like we do in  the first three cases or by adding two  quanta along two different directions as  we do in these other three cases  so let's finish by generalizing these  results  what is the degeneracy of a general  energy eigenvalue e n  we need to figure out how many triplets  n x and y and n z are such that their  sum is equal to n  to figure it out let's first fix n x  then n y plus and z must be equal to n  minus nx  how many choices do we have for ny and  nz  the first is that ny is zero and then nz  must be n minus nx  the second is that ny is one and then nz  must be n minus nx minus one and so on  all the way to ny being n minus nx and  nz being zero  if we consider all of these  possibilities we get a total of n minus  nx plus one terms  this means that the degeneracy gn of  state n is given by the sum over all  possible choices of an x and we've just  figured out that for every such choice  we have n minus nx plus one terms as  possible choices of n y and then z  n and one can be taken out of the sum so  we end up with this term  minus the sum over an x  this is just n plus one  and this is a standard arithmetic series  which is equal to this  putting everything together we end up  with one half times n plus one times n  plus two  and this is our big result the energy  eigenvalue e n of the isotropic three  dimensional quantum harmonic oscillator  has degeneracy one half times n plus one  times n plus two  you should convince yourself that this  general result is really consistent with  the three examples that we've discussed  but more generally we see that the  higher the energy level the larger the  degeneracy this again arises from the  fact that the energy levels in a  harmonic oscillator are equally spaced  and that in the isotropic oscillator the  spacing is the same in all three spatial  dimensions  now conceptually the higher the energy  the more ways we can distribute it  amongst the three directions  i want to finish with two brief points  first there are some very interesting  underlying symmetries that lead to  degeneracies of the isotropic quantum  harmonic oscillator that we've explored  today and we may get into that in a  future video  second as the energy eigenvalues are  degenerate this means that the  corresponding eigenstates are not  uniquely defined  it is actually interesting to explore  alternative choices for the energy  eigenstates and we describe a different  approach to the isotropic  three-dimensional harmonic oscillator in  the context of central potentials so i  encourage you to check out those  videos the isotropic three-dimensional  quantum harmonic oscillator has a highly  degenerate energy spectrum among other  things what this means is that there  isn't a unique choice for the energy  eigenstates in any given degenerate  subspace in fact in our videos we find  isotropic harmonic oscillators in a  different context as well when we study  central potentials i therefore really  encourage you to check out those videos  as well because there we write down the  energy eigenstates in a completely  different way and as always if you liked  the video please subscribe
 

